Flow Augmentation
Suppose \(f\) is a flow in \(G\) and \(f'\) is a flow in the residual network \(G_f\).
\[
(f \uparrow f')(u, v) = \begin{cases}
    f(u, v) + f'(u, v) - f'(v, u) & \text{if } (u, v) \in E \\
    0                              & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\(|f \uparrow f'| = |f| + |f'|\).

This shows augmentation adds the residual flow value to the original flow.

Running Time of Ford-Fulkerson
Let \(n\) be the number of nodes and \(m\) be the number of edges in \(G\).

Since \(m \ge n/2\), we simplify \(O(m+n) = O(m)\).{.fragment}
The while loop runs at most \(f^*\) iterations where \(f^*\) is the max flow value.{.fragment}
In each iteration, a path is found in the residual graph (\(\le 2m\) edges).{.fragment}
Using DFS/BFS: finding path \(P\) takes \(O(m)\) time.{.fragment}
Augmenting takes \(O(n)\) time (at most \(n-1\) edges).{.fragment}
New residual graph can be constructed in \(O(m)\) time.{.fragment}


Total time complexity of Ford-Fulkerson: \(O(m f^*)\)

Problem: \(f^*\) could be very large — algorithm is pseudo-polynomial!{.fragment}

The Edmonds-Karp Insight

Published by Jack Edmonds and Richard Karp in 1972 (independently discovered by Yefim Dinitz in 1970){.fragment}
It specifies exactly how to find the augmenting path{.fragment}


The Rule: Always choose the augmenting path with the fewest number of edges — i.e., use BFS instead of DFS.







Note


Key idea: By always picking the shortest augmenting path, we guarantee that the number of augmentations is polynomial (\(O(VE)\)).

Residual Networks Review
The residual network \(G_f\) tells us where we can push more flow:

Forward edges: \(c_f(u, v) = c(u, v) - f(u, v)\) (remaining capacity){.fragment}
Backward edges: \(c_f(v, u) = f(u, v)\) (allows “undoing” flow){.fragment}
BFS runs on \(G_f\), looking for any path from \(s\) to \(t\) where every edge has \(c_f &gt; 0\){.fragment}

The distance from \(s\) to any node in \(G_f\) is measured in number of edges (unweighted BFS).

Time Complexity

Total Time Complexity: \(\mathcal{O}(V E^2)\)

Breakdown: - BFS Cost: Finding a shortest path takes \(\mathcal{O}(E)\) time{.fragment} - Number of Augmentations: The key theorem states the total number of flow augmentations is bounded by \(\mathcal{O}(VE)\){.fragment} - Why? The shortest path distance from \(s\) to any node in \(G_f\) increases monotonically. Each edge can become a “bottleneck” at most \(V/2\) times.{.fragment} - \(\mathcal{O}(E)\) per BFS \(\times\) \(\mathcal{O}(VE)\) augmentations = \(\mathcal{O}(V E^2)\){.fragment}

Key Lemma: Distance Monotonicity

During Edmonds-Karp, the distance \(\delta_f(v)\) from \(s\) to any node \(v\) in the residual graph \(G_f\) never decreases — it increases monotonically.

Proof sketch: - Suppose a later augmentation decreased \(\delta_f(v)\) to some value \(d' &lt; d\){.fragment} - Consider the first such node \(v\) and the first time its distance drops{.fragment} - Show a contradiction by analyzing the edge \((u,v)\) on the new shortest path{.fragment}
Consequence: Each edge can be a bottleneck (determine \(c_f(p)\)) at most \(O(V)\) times. Since there are \(O(E)\) edges, total augmentations = \(O(VE)\).✓{.fragment}

Bipartite Matching


0.5

Suppose we have two sets: \(L\) (people) and \(R\) (jobs){.fragment}
A person can do more than one job{.fragment}
Model this as a bipartite graph{.fragment}


0.5

Matching Definition


0.5

A matching gives an assignment of people to tasks such that:

A person can do at most one task{.fragment}
No two people share a task{.fragment}

Formally: A matching is a subset of edges \(M \subseteq E\) such that for all \(v \in V\), at most one edge of \(M\) is incident on \(v\).{.fragment}


0.5

Construction Summary
Reduction steps: 1. Direct the edges from \(L\) to \(R\){.fragment} 2. Add new vertices \(s\) and \(t\){.fragment} 3. Add edge \(s \to v\) for every \(v \in L\){.fragment} 4. Add edge \(v \to t\) for every \(v \in R\){.fragment} 5. Make all capacities \(1\){.fragment}
Result: Edges used in the maximum flow correspond to the maximum matching.{.fragment}

Correctness

Since capacities are integral, max flow will be integral{.fragment}
Because capacities are \(1\): either use an edge fully or not at all{.fragment}

We show: - \(M\) is a matching: At most one edge leaves any node in \(L\), at most one enters any node in \(R\).{.fragment} - \(M\) is maximum: If a larger matching \(M'\) existed, then \(f^*\) (max flow value) would not be maximum — contradiction.{.fragment}

\(M\) is a Matching




Matching property




Capacity constraints on \(s \to L\) edges: at most 1 unit leaves each \(L\)-node.{.fragment}
Capacity constraints on \(R \to t\) edges: at most 1 unit enters each \(R\)-node.{.fragment}
Thus the flow-carrying edges form a matching.{.fragment}

\(M\) is Maximum

Suppose we find a maximum flow \(f^* = k\).{.fragment}
This corresponds to a matching \(M\) of size \(k\).{.fragment}
Suppose \(M\) is not maximum, and there exists another matching \(M'\) of size \(&gt; k\).{.fragment}
Then \(M'\) would give a flow of value \(&gt; k\), contradicting that \(f^*\) is a maximum flow.{.fragment}
Therefore \(M\) is maximum. ✓{.fragment}

Time Complexity for Bipartite Matching

Maximum value of the flow is \(n\) where \(|L| = n\){.fragment}
Number of edges in \(G'\) is \(m + 2n\) where \(m\) is edges in original \(G\){.fragment}
Using Ford-Fulkerson: \(O(mn)\) time{.fragment}







Important


Bipartite matching is a beautiful application of network flow — showing how a seemingly unrelated problem can be solved via reduction.

Why Randomization?
Randomized algorithms use random choices during execution to achieve:

Simplicity: Often simpler than deterministic alternatives{.fragment}
Efficiency: Can be asymptotically faster{.fragment}
Correctness: May err with tiny controlled probability{.fragment}

Two flavors: - Las Vegas: Always correct, expected runtime small (e.g., Randomized Quicksort){.fragment} - Monte Carlo: Runtime always small, may err with bounded probability{.fragment}

Randomized Protocol: Fingerprinting
Idea: Use random fingerprints of the strings.
Protocol: 1. Alice picks a random prime \(p\) from \(\{2, \dots, P\}\) (where \(P\) is sufficiently large){.fragment} 2. Alice sends Bob: the prime \(p\) and \(x \bmod p\){.fragment} 3. Bob checks if \(x \bmod p \equiv y \bmod p\){.fragment}
Communication: \(O(\log P)\) bits for the prime + \(O(\log p)\) bits for the fingerprint = \(O(\log n)\) bits.{.fragment}

Analysis: Correctness
If \(x = y\): Alice and Bob always output EQUAL. ✓{.fragment}
If \(x \neq y\): Bob might output EQUAL if \(x \equiv y \pmod p\) — a false positive.{.fragment}
How many primes \(p\) can cause \(x \equiv y \pmod p\)?

If \(x \neq y\), then difference \(D = |x - y|\) is some integer of at most \(n\) bits{.fragment}
Primes dividing \(D\) are \(&lt; n \log n\) in count (since \(D\) has at most \(n\) bits, it has \(&lt; n \log n\) distinct prime factors){.fragment}
Choose \(P\) so the range \(\{2, \dots, P\}\) contains \(t \gg n \log n\) primes{.fragment}

Error Probability

By choosing \(P\) such that there are at least \(t \ge n^2\) primes in \(\{2, \dots, P\}\), the error probability is at most \(1/n\).

Proof: - At most \(n \log n\) “bad” primes divide \(D = |x - y|\){.fragment} - Total primes in range: \(\ge n^2\){.fragment} - Error probability \(\le \frac{n \log n}{n^2} = \frac{\log n}{n} = O(1/n)\){.fragment}
Communication cost: \(O(\log n)\) bits — exponentially better than deterministic!{.fragment}

Freivalds’: Error Analysis
If \(C = AB\): Algorithm always outputs CORRECT. ✓{.fragment}
If \(C \neq AB\): Let \(D = AB - C \neq 0\). Then \(D \vec{r} \neq 0\) with probability \(\ge 1/2\).{.fragment}
Why? Since \(D \neq 0\), some row \(d^T\) is non-zero. For a random \(\vec{r} \in \{0,1\}^n\): - \(d^T \vec{r} = 0\) only if half the entries of \(d\) happen to cancel — probability \(\le 1/2\){.fragment}
Error bound: \(P(\text{false positive}) \le 1/2\) — repeat \(k\) times to get error \(\le 2^{-k}\){.fragment}

Summary

Randomized algorithms use random bits to achieve efficiency or simplicity{.fragment}
Las Vegas vs. Monte Carlo: distinguish by whether errors occur{.fragment}
Fingerprinting: A general technique for testing equality with small communication{.fragment}
Freivalds’ check: Matrix product verification in \(O(n^2)\) via random vectors{.fragment}
Karger’s min-cut: Elegant edge-contraction algorithm for global min cut{.fragment}

Randomization is a central theme in modern algorithm design — we will see more applications in later lectures!

Introduction
Given a problem \(X\) and a machine model \(M\), how long does it take to solve \(X\) using a machine from \(M\)?{.fragment}
Instead of answering this difficult question directly, we ask:{.fragment}

How can we classify problems into complexity classes based on their apparent difficulty?{.fragment}
What relationships can we establish between these classes?{.fragment}

The most famous of these questions: P vs. NP.{.fragment}

Models of Computation
Different models capture different aspects of computation:

Logic Circuit: Consists of logic gates realizing Boolean functions — basic building blocks of all digital computers{.fragment}
Finite-State Machine (FSM): A machine with memory. Executes steps taking current state and external input to produce output.{.fragment}
Random-Access Machine (RAM): A CPU with random-access memory — holds data and programs (instructions for the CPU).{.fragment}
Other models: Push-down automata, Turing machines{.fragment}







Note


All reasonable models are polynomially equivalent — a problem solvable in polynomial time on one is solvable in polynomial time on another.

Decision vs. Optimization Problems
For a problem \(X\):

Search Problem: Find a solution that satisfies \(X\){.fragment}
Optimization Problem: Find a solution that satisfies \(X\) and minimizes (maximizes) an objective function{.fragment}
Decision Problem: Determine if there exists any solution for \(X\){.fragment}

Example: SHORTEST PATH (Optimization) vs. PATH (Decision): - SHORTEST PATH: Find a path from \(u\) to \(v\) using the fewest edges - PATH: Does a path exist from \(u\) to \(v\) consisting of at most \(k\) edges?






Important


Decision problems are easier to reason about mathematically and capture the essential difficulty of most problems.

Problems and Languages
Most problems studied in complexity theory are decision problems.

Given an input \(x\), answer “yes” or “no” based on whether \(x\) satisfies some predicate \(P\){.fragment}
Example: GRAPH 3-COLORABILITY: Given a graph \(G\), can we assign three colors to vertices so no edge has two endpoints of the same color?{.fragment}

Formal definition: Inputs are encoded over alphabet \(\Sigma\) (typically \(\{0,1\}\)). A language \(L\) is a set of finite strings over \(\Sigma\). If \(x \in L\), output “yes”; otherwise “no”.{.fragment}

The Class NP

NP (Non-deterministic Polynomial) = All decision problems that can be verified in polynomial time.

A problem \(D\) is in NP if there exists a verifier \(V\) taking two inputs \(x\) (instance) and \(c\) (certificate) such that:

For all yes-instances \(x\), there exists \(c\) such that \(V(x, c)\) outputs yes{.fragment}
For all no-instances \(x\), for all \(c\), \(V(x, c)\) outputs no{.fragment}
\(V\) runs in time polynomial in \(|x|\){.fragment}

Key intuition: NP = problems where solutions are easy to check, even if hard to find.

P vs. NP
The central open question in computer science:






Important


P vs. NP: Is P = NP?




P ⊆ NP trivially: if we can solve a problem in polynomial time, we can verify solutions in polynomial time (just solve and compare).{.fragment}
Does NP ⊆ P? Are there problems whose solutions are easy to check but inherently hard to find?{.fragment}
If \(P = NP\), many hard problems (TSP, scheduling, protein folding) would have efficient solutions.{.fragment}
Most researchers believe \(P \neq NP\), but no proof exists.{.fragment}

$1,000,000 question: One of the seven Millenium Prize Problems.

Recap: The Big Picture

P: Problems solvable in polynomial time (efficient){.fragment}
NP: Problems whose solutions can be verified in polynomial time{.fragment}
NP-Complete: The hardest problems in NP — if any one is in P, then P = NP{.fragment}

Our goal: Learn how to prove a problem is NP-complete using reductions.{.fragment}

Proving NP-Completeness: Recipe
To show \(X\) is NP-complete:

Step 1: Show \(X\) is in NP — design a polynomial-time verifier{.fragment}
Step 2: Pick a known NP-complete problem \(Y\){.fragment}
Step 3: Construct a polynomial-time reduction from \(Y\) to \(X\): \(Y \le_p X\){.fragment}

Transform any instance of \(Y\) into an instance of \(X\)
Ensure YES-instances map to YES-instances, NO to NO

Step 4: Prove correctness of the reduction{.fragment}







Important


Direction matters: Reduce FROM a known hard problem TO your problem to show your problem is hard.

The Reduction Template
For most 3-SAT reductions, we follow this pattern:

For each clause \((\ell_1 \lor \ell_2 \lor \ell_3)\), create a gadget in your problem{.fragment}
Encode the “one literal per clause must be true” constraint in the gadget{.fragment}
Encode consistency constraints (a variable and its negation can’t both be true){.fragment}
Set a parameter \(k\) equal to the number of clauses{.fragment}

The reduction is correct if: - (\(\Rightarrow\)) A satisfying assignment \(\implies\) a solution of size \(k\) - (\(\Leftarrow\)) A solution of size \(k\) \(\implies\) a satisfying assignment

IND-SET is NP-hard: Reduction from 3-SAT
Given a 3-CNF formula \(\phi\) with \(k\) clauses, construct graph \(G\):

For each clause, create a triangle of 3 vertices (one per literal){.fragment}
For each pair of opposite literals (e.g., \(x_i\) and \(\overline{x_i}\)), add an edge between them{.fragment}

Claim: \(\phi\) is satisfiable \(\iff\) \(G\) has an independent set of size \(k\).
3-SAT to IND-SET reduction.

IND-SET: Proof of Correctness
(\(\Rightarrow\)) If \(\phi\) is satisfiable: - In each clause, at least one literal is true{.fragment} - Pick one true literal per clause — this gives \(k\) vertices{.fragment} - No edge within a triangle (only one per triangle picked) ✓{.fragment} - No edge between opposite literals (both can’t be true) ✓{.fragment} - So these \(k\) vertices form an independent set ✓{.fragment}
(\(\Leftarrow\)) If \(G\) has an independent set \(S\) of size \(k\): - \(S\) must contain exactly one vertex from each triangle (since triangles have 3 vertices but edges prevent picking more){.fragment} - No two opposite literals are in \(S\){.fragment} - Set each literal in \(S\) to true → each clause satisfied → \(\phi\) is satisfiable ✓{.fragment}

What We Need to Prove
To show SAT is NP-complete, we need:

SAT is in NP: Given a satisfying assignment, verify it in polynomial time ✓ (trivial — just evaluate the formula){.fragment}
SAT is NP-hard: For every problem \(D\) in NP, \(D \le_p\) SAT{.fragment}

The hard part: Show that any NP problem can be reduced to SAT.

Building the Formula
The formula \(\phi\) is the AND of several constraint clauses:

Initial condition: Tape starts with input \(x\), head at position 0, state \(q_0\){.fragment}
Final condition: Machine reaches \(q_{accept}\) at some time \(\le p(n)\){.fragment}
Exactly one: At each time, the machine is in exactly one state, head is at exactly one position, each cell has exactly one symbol{.fragment}
Transition: Each configuration follows from the previous via a valid transition of the NDTM{.fragment}

Implications
The Cook-Levin theorem has profound implications:

SAT is the “hardest” problem in NP — every NP problem can be reduced to SAT{.fragment}
Start of NP-completeness theory — by reducing SAT to other problems, we proved many problems NP-complete (Karp’s 21 problems){.fragment}
If SAT ∈ P, then P = NP — one of the biggest open questions{.fragment}
Practical impact: SAT solvers are now incredibly efficient, solving industrial instances with millions of variables{.fragment}

From SAT to 3-SAT
SAT can be reduced to 3-SAT (each clause has \(\le 3\) literals):

Any clause with \(k &gt; 3\) literals can be broken into \(k-2\) clauses of 3 literals using auxiliary variables{.fragment}

Example: \((a \lor b \lor c \lor d \lor e)\) becomes: \[(a \lor b \lor z_1) \land (\overline{z_1} \lor c \lor z_2) \land (\overline{z_2} \lor d \lor e)\] {.fragment}

Each clause of size 1 or 2 can be padded to size 3{.fragment}

Result: 3-SAT is also NP-complete, and is more convenient for reductions{.fragment}

Overview

Time complexity classes categorize problems by the resources needed to solve them{.fragment}
P: Problems solvable in polynomial time{.fragment}
NP: Decision problems solvable in polynomial time via a “lucky” algorithm (verifiable in polynomial time){.fragment}
EXP: Problems solvable in exponential time{.fragment}
R: Problems solvable in finite time (“recursive” language){.fragment}

The Halting Problem

The Halting Problem: Given a computer program \(P\) and input \(I\), does \(P\) ever halt when run on \(I\)?


Uncomputable (not in R): There is no algorithm for solving this{.fragment}
It is a decision problem — the answer is either YES or NO{.fragment}
Proved by Alan Turing (and Alonzo Church) in 1936{.fragment}
This is the canonical example of an undecidable problem{.fragment}

Proving NP-Completeness: Recipe
To show \(X\) is NP-complete:

Step 1: Show \(X\) is in NP — design a polynomial-time verifier{.fragment}
Step 2: Pick a known NP-complete problem \(Y\){.fragment}
Step 3: Construct a polynomial-time reduction from \(Y\) to \(X\): \(Y \le_p X\){.fragment}
Step 4: Prove correctness (both directions){.fragment}

Key source problems: - CIRCUIT-SAT — the first NP-complete problem - 3-SAT — most common starting point for reductions - IND-SET, VERTEX COVER, CLIQUE — problems useful as intermediate steps

CIRCUIT-SAT

CIRCUIT-SAT - Input: An acyclic circuit \(C\) of logic gates with a single output - Answer: YES if there is a setting of the inputs that causes \(C\) to output 1; NO otherwise


First problem proven NP-complete (Cook-Levin Theorem){.fragment}
By reducing CIRCUIT-SAT to other problems, we chain the NP-completeness{.fragment}

3-SAT

3-SAT - Input: A CNF formula (AND of ORs) over \(n\) variables where each clause has at most 3 literals - Answer: YES if there is a satisfying assignment; NO otherwise


3-SAT is NP-complete (reduction from CIRCUIT-SAT via clause splitting){.fragment}
Most common starting point for NP-completeness proofs{.fragment}

IND-SET NP-hardness: Reduction Details
Given a 3-CNF formula \(\phi\) with \(k\) clauses, build \(G\):

\(3k\) vertices (one per literal occurrence){.fragment}
Each clause contributes 3 edges connecting its literals (forming a triangle){.fragment}
For each pair of opposite literals (e.g., \(x_i\) and \(\overline{x_i}\)), add an edge{.fragment}

Claim: \(\phi\) satisfiable \(\iff\) \(G\) has independent set of size \(k\) - (\(\Rightarrow\)) Pick one true literal per clause → \(k\) vertices, no conflicts{.fragment} - (\(\Leftarrow\)) \(k\)-independent set picks one vertex per triangle → set those literals true → \(\phi\) satisfied{.fragment}

Today: Two Classic NP-Completeness Proofs

CLIQUE — reduction from 3-SAT{.fragment}
VERTEX COVER (VC) — reduction from IND-SET{.fragment}







Note


We’ll see two different reduction patterns: 3-SAT to a graph problem, and a simple direct reduction between graph problems.

CLIQUE is in NP
Given a graph \(G = (V, E)\), a parameter \(k\), and a vertex subset \(C\) (certificate):

Check if \(|C| \ge k\) — \(O(|V|)\) time{.fragment}
Check that \(C\) forms a clique — verify every pair of vertices in \(C\) is adjacent: \(O(|C|^2) = O(|V|^2)\) time{.fragment}

Thus CLIQUE is in NP. ✓{.fragment}

CLIQUE NP-hardness: Proof (\(\Rightarrow\))
Suppose formula \(F\) is satisfiable.

In the satisfying assignment, at least one literal is true in every clause{.fragment}
In each triple \(T_c\) of \(G\), select one node corresponding to a true literal{.fragment}
These \(k\) nodes form a \(k\)-clique:{.fragment}

Only one node per triple (no same-triple conflict) ✓
No complementary labels (both literals can’t be true) ✓
All selected nodes are connected (edges exist between any two compatible literals){.fragment}


Thus a satisfying assignment gives a clique of size \(k\). ✓

CLIQUE NP-hardness: Proof (\(\Leftarrow\))
Suppose \(G\) has a \(k\)-clique.

No two clique nodes occur in the same triple (no internal edges within a triple){.fragment}
Since there are \(k\) triples and \(k\) clique nodes, each triple contributes exactly one{.fragment}
No two clique nodes are labeled with complementary literals (no edge between them){.fragment}
Assign truth values: set each literal labeling a clique node to true{.fragment}
Since each triple contains a clique node, each clause has a true literal → \(F\) is satisfiable ✓{.fragment}

VC is in NP
Given \(G = (V, E)\), integer \(k\), and a set \(S\) (certificate):

Check \(|S| \le k\) — \(O(|V|)\) time{.fragment}
Verify \(S\) covers all edges: for each \((u,v) \in E\), check \(u \in S\) or \(v \in S\) — \(O(|E|)\) time{.fragment}

Thus VERTEX COVER is in NP. ✓{.fragment}

VC NP-hardness: Proof

Proof. (\(\Rightarrow\)) Suppose \(S\) is an independent set of size \(k\) in \(G\).

Since \(S\) has no internal edges, every edge must have at least one endpoint in \(V \setminus S\){.fragment}
Thus \(V \setminus S\) is a vertex cover of size \(|V| - k\) ✓{.fragment}

(\(\Leftarrow\)) Suppose \(S\) is a vertex cover of size \(k'\) in \(G'\).

Every edge has an endpoint in \(S\), so no edge has both endpoints in \(V \setminus S\){.fragment}
Thus \(V \setminus S\) is an independent set of size \(|V| - k' = k\) ✓{.fragment}








Important


This is a beautiful example of a complementary relation: \(S\) is an independent set iff \(V \setminus S\) is a vertex cover.

What to Do When Problems Are NP-Hard?
When we face an NP-hard optimization problem, we have options:

Exact Algorithm: Guarantees optimal solution. Runtime may not be polynomial, but works for instances of moderate size.{.fragment}
Heuristic: Fast algorithm with no formal guarantees on solution quality.{.fragment}
Approximation Algorithm: Polynomial-time algorithm that guarantees a solution within a known factor of optimal.{.fragment}

We focus on option 3 — the best of both worlds!{.fragment}

Proving Approximation Guarantees
For a \(\rho\)-approximation algorithm ALG we must:

Describe the algorithm clearly{.fragment}
Analyze its running time (should be polynomial){.fragment}
Prove that \(\text{ALG}(I) \le \rho \cdot \text{OPT}(I)\) for all inputs \(I\){.fragment}

The Challenge: How to bound \(\text{ALG}(I)\) in terms of \(\text{OPT}(I)\) when we don’t know OPT?
Solution: Find a lower bound on OPT that we can compute easily.{.fragment}
Example: If we can prove \(\text{OPT} \ge 100\) and \(\text{ALG} \le 200\), then \(\text{ALG} \le 2 \cdot \text{OPT}\).

Recap

Approximation algorithms provide quality guarantees for NP-hard optimization problems{.fragment}
\(\rho\)-approximation: solution is within factor \(\rho\) of optimal{.fragment}
PTAS vs FPTAS: trade-off between runtime and approximation quality{.fragment}

Vertex Cover: Detailed Analysis
APPROX-VERTEX-COVER (review): - Pick any edge \((u,v)\), add both \(u\) and \(v\) to cover{.fragment} - Remove all incident edges{.fragment} - Repeat until no edges remain{.fragment}
Why is this a 2-approximation?{.fragment} - The set of picked edges forms a matching \(M\) — no two share a vertex{.fragment} - Any vertex cover must include at least one endpoint of each edge in \(M\) (otherwise that edge is uncovered){.fragment} - So \(|\text{OPT}| \ge |M|\){.fragment} - Our algorithm picks both endpoints: \(|\text{ALG}| = 2|M| \le 2|\text{OPT}|\) ✓{.fragment}

Hardness of Approximation: General TSP

General TSP cannot be approximated within any polynomial factor (unless P = NP).

Proof Idea: Reduction from Hamiltonian Cycle:

Given a graph \(G = (V, E)\) (Hamiltonian Cycle instance), construct a complete graph \(G'\) with costs:{.fragment}

If \((u,v) \in E\): \(c(u,v) = 1\){.fragment}
If \((u,v) \notin E\): \(c(u,v) = \text{(large)}\), say \(L = n \cdot \rho\){.fragment}


If \(G\) has a Hamiltonian cycle: optimal tour cost = \(n\){.fragment} If \(G\) has no Hamiltonian cycle: any tour uses a non-edge, cost \(\ge (n-1) + L &gt; \rho n\){.fragment}
Thus \(\rho\)-approximation could distinguish the two — impossible unless P = NP.{.fragment}

2-Approximation Proof

APPROX-TSP-TOUR is a polynomial-time 2-approximation algorithm for Metric TSP.

Proof: - Let \(H^*\) be the optimal tour. - Remove an arbitrary edge from \(H^*\) — this yields a spanning tree \(T\) with \(c(T) \le c(H^*)\) (non-negative costs){.fragment} - Since \(T_{\min}\) is an MST: \(c(T_{\min}) \le c(T) \le c(H^*)\){.fragment} - The full walk \(W\) traverses each edge twice: \(c(W) = 2c(T_{\min}) \le 2c(H^*)\){.fragment} - Shortcutting duplicates from \(W\) gives tour \(H\) with \(c(H) \le c(W)\) (by triangle inequality){.fragment} - Therefore: \(c(H) \le c(W) \le 2c(H^*)\) ✓{.fragment}
